Tap timings as macOS has them, and no taps right after a click.

The timings of macOS 27.2's tap handling all come from one table in
HSTouchHIDService (_gTimingPrefs_ at 0x16a858, loaded 91 times and never
written; MTParameterFactory_::initGestureTimingParams is an empty function):

- A tap must lift within 250 ms of its newest touchdown (+0x30 = 0.25 s,
  MTChordCycling_::tapHasValidTimingAndStrength 0x879cc). libinput gave
  180 ms (DEFAULT_TAP_TIMEOUT_PERIOD), which also sets when a resting finger
  becomes a hold.
- Tap-and-drag: after a tap macOS waits 200 ms (+0x40,
  MTTapDragManager_::sendWaitingClickAtHalfTimeout 0x6be78) for the finger to
  come back; then the button goes down and moving drags. libinput's one-
  finger window was 160 + 20 ms; the base is now 180 ms, so one finger gets
  200 ms (macOS only has one-finger tap-and-drag; two and three fingers keep
  libinput's 20 ms steps on top).
- A tap-and-drag survives 500 ms after the finger lifts (+0x48,
  MTTapDragManager_::halfTapDragTimedOut 0x6bc84): libinput's drag lock in
  timeout mode, whose timeout was 300 ms. (Without "drag lock" macOS behaves
  like that mode; with it, like the sticky mode.)
- No tap counts if it lifts within 250 ms of the physical button going down
  or up: -[MTTrackpadUberAlg setDivingButtonState:] (0x8aa4c) stamps every
  unblocked button change into the hand statistics' t_recentButtonActivity
  (+0x30), and tapHasValidTimingAndStrength rejects a tap when that stamp is
  under 0.25 s old (0x87a84..0x87a98). libinput only killed taps while the
  button was down. A touch lifting that soon after a button change now
  takes the tap state machine to DEAD first, as a button press does.

Left as libinput has them: the 1.3 mm tap movement limit (macOS counts 10
emitted 1/400 in counts for one finger, 5 for two, after the 0.169 mm
deadband and the touchdown settling filter that libinput does not have, and
also wants the fastest finger under 80 mm/s at the lift), the 20 ms minimum
tap length and the 125 ms hover-to-touch limit.

--- a/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-tap.c
+++ b/src/evdev-mt-touchpad-tap.c
@@ -29,10 +29,16 @@
 
 #include "evdev-mt-touchpad.h"
 
-#define DEFAULT_TAP_TIMEOUT_PERIOD usec_from_millis(180)
-#define DEFAULT_DRAG_TIMEOUT_PERIOD_BASE usec_from_millis(160)
+/* macOS 27.2 (HSTouchHIDService, the _gTimingPrefs_ table at 0x16a858): a tap
+ * lifts within 250 ms of its newest touchdown (+0x30), a tap-and-drag needs the
+ * finger back within 200 ms of the tap's lift (+0x40), a drag survives 500 ms
+ * after the finger lifts (+0x48), and no tap counts within 250 ms of the
+ * physical button going down or up (+0x30, against t_recentButtonActivity). */
+#define DEFAULT_TAP_TIMEOUT_PERIOD usec_from_millis(250)
+#define DEFAULT_DRAG_TIMEOUT_PERIOD_BASE usec_from_millis(180)
 #define DEFAULT_DRAG_TIMEOUT_PERIOD_PERFINGER usec_from_millis(20)
-#define DEFAULT_DRAGLOCK_TIMEOUT_PERIOD usec_from_millis(300)
+#define DEFAULT_DRAGLOCK_TIMEOUT_PERIOD usec_from_millis(500)
+#define DEFAULT_TAP_BUTTON_QUIET_PERIOD usec_from_millis(250)
 #define DEFAULT_TAP_MOVE_THRESHOLD 1.3 /* mm */
 
 enum tap_event {
@@ -1221,6 +1227,10 @@
 	if (tp->buttons.is_clickpad && tp->queued & TOUCHPAD_EVENT_BUTTON_PRESS)
 		tp_tap_handle_event(tp, NULL, TAP_EVENT_BUTTON, time);
 
+	if (tp->buttons.is_clickpad &&
+	    tp->queued & (TOUCHPAD_EVENT_BUTTON_PRESS | TOUCHPAD_EVENT_BUTTON_RELEASE))
+		tp->tap.button_time = time;
+
 	tp_for_each_touch(tp, t) {
 		if (!t->dirty || t->state == TOUCH_NONE)
 			continue;
@@ -1271,6 +1281,13 @@
 		} else if (t->state == TOUCH_END) {
 			if (t->was_down) {
 				assert(tp->tap.nfingers_down >= 1);
+				/* As on macOS, a touch lifting this soon after the
+				 * button went down or up is no tap */
+				if (tp->buttons.is_clickpad &&
+				    usec_cmp(time,
+					     usec_add(tp->tap.button_time,
+						      DEFAULT_TAP_BUTTON_QUIET_PERIOD)) < 0)
+					tp_tap_handle_event(tp, NULL, TAP_EVENT_BUTTON, time);
 				tp->tap.nfingers_down--;
 				tp_tap_handle_event(tp, t, TAP_EVENT_RELEASE, time);
 			}
